{
  "rules": [
    "Strong upward-trend underprediction: when the last 5–10 days are rising, the model systematically forecasts too low and too flat. Apply an additive uplift that increases with horizon (small in days 1–3, large by days 10–30).",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: errors grow with lead time. Treat forecasts beyond ~7–10 days as having a larger positive bias correction (true outcomes run materially above model, especially days 10–30).",
    "Trend/level lag: the model reacts slowly to regime shifts (breakout from mid-60s to 70s/80s). Use a momentum-based adjustment (e.g., add a fraction of recent 5–10d slope to all future days).",
    "Over-mean-reversion after strength: the model repeatedly pulls back toward ~69–72 even while the market continues trending up. Reduce mean-reversion strength (or cap downward adjustments) when price is making higher highs/higher lows.",
    "Volatility underestimation: true outcomes show larger swings and higher realized levels during rallies. Inflate forecast volatility upward in strong-trend regimes (wider bands; higher path).",
    "Late-horizon ceiling effect: many forecasts cluster around ~70–73 while realized prices reach ~80–83. If forecast path plateaus while recent trend is positive, force a rising terminal level (raise days 20–30 more aggressively)."
  ]
}
